1
00:00:08,450 --> 00:00:14,800
A delivery box is 30 centimetres long, 20 centimetres wide and 10 centimetres high.

2
00:00:14,850 --> 00:00:17,660
You want to fill it with sugar cubes.

3
00:00:17,710 --> 00:00:21,520
Each cube is 1 centimetre along every edge.

4
00:00:21,570 --> 00:00:23,930
How many sugar cubes fit in the box?

5
00:00:23,980 --> 00:00:27,480
Pause the video and work it out.

6
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:29,910
Fill the bottom layer first.

7
00:00:29,960 --> 00:00:32,260
It has 30 rows of 20 cubes.

8
00:00:32,310 --> 00:00:34,370
That is 600 cubes.

9
00:00:34,420 --> 00:00:39,580
The box is 10 centimetres high, so there are 10 layers.

10
00:00:39,630 --> 00:00:42,780
600 times 10 is 6,000 cubes.

11
00:00:42,830 --> 00:00:47,730
Remember: the area of a rectangle is the length times the width.

12
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:52,690
12 centimetres by 5 centimetres is 60 square centimetres.

13
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:56,510
Volume is the amount of space that a 3D shape takes up.

14
00:00:56,560 --> 00:01:00,950
We measure it in cubic units, such as cubic centimetres.

15
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:05,530
For a cuboid, multiply the length, the width and the height.

16
00:01:05,580 --> 00:01:07,240
A cube has 3 equal edges.

17
00:01:07,290 --> 00:01:11,750
So its volume is the side, times the side, times the side.

18
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:12,450
Your turn.

19
00:01:12,500 --> 00:01:16,440
What is the volume of a cube with sides of 5 centimetres?

20
00:01:16,490 --> 00:01:19,730
Pause the video and try it.

21
00:01:19,780 --> 00:01:21,370
5 times 5 is 25.

22
00:01:21,420 --> 00:01:23,620
And 25 times 5 is 125.

23
00:01:23,670 --> 00:01:27,850
The volume is 125 cubic centimetres.

24
00:01:27,900 --> 00:01:30,960
Capacity is how much a container holds.

25
00:01:31,010 --> 00:01:33,990
1 cubic centimetre holds 1 millilitre.

26
00:01:34,040 --> 00:01:37,730
So 1,000 cubic centimetres hold 1 litre.

27
00:01:37,780 --> 00:01:42,210
Our delivery box has a volume of 6,000 cubic centimetres.

28
00:01:42,260 --> 00:01:45,510
So it could hold 6 litres of water.

29
00:01:45,560 --> 00:01:46,310
Your turn.

30
00:01:46,360 --> 00:01:49,720
How many litres is 4,500 cubic centimetres?

31
00:01:49,770 --> 00:01:53,260
Pause the video and try it.

32
00:01:53,310 --> 00:01:55,120
Divide by 1,000.

33
00:01:55,170 --> 00:01:59,980
4,500 divided by 1,000 is 4.5 litres.

34
00:02:00,030 --> 00:02:01,330
Here is the method.

35
00:02:01,380 --> 00:02:05,810
Step 1: check that all 3 lengths are in the same units.

36
00:02:05,860 --> 00:02:10,980
Step 2: multiply the length, the width and the height, in any order.

37
00:02:11,030 --> 00:02:14,410
Step 3: give the answer in cubic units.

38
00:02:14,460 --> 00:02:20,930
Step 4: for capacity, remember that 1,000 cubic centimetres is 1 litre.

39
00:02:20,980 --> 00:02:27,830
Find the volume of a cuboid that is 9 centimetres by 4 centimetres by 5 centimetres.

40
00:02:27,880 --> 00:02:29,450
9 times 4 is 36.

41
00:02:29,500 --> 00:02:31,680
And 36 times 5 is 180.

42
00:02:31,730 --> 00:02:35,890
The volume is 180 cubic centimetres.

43
00:02:35,940 --> 00:02:41,930
A fish tank is 1.5 metres long, 40 centimetres wide and 30 centimetres high.

44
00:02:41,980 --> 00:02:44,780
How many litres does it hold?

45
00:02:44,830 --> 00:02:46,780
Use the same units.

46
00:02:46,830 --> 00:02:49,920
1.5 metres is 150 centimetres.

47
00:02:49,970 --> 00:02:55,820
150 times 40 times 30 is 180,000 cubic centimetres.

48
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:57,420
Divide by 1,000.

49
00:02:57,470 --> 00:03:00,570
The tank holds 180 litres.

50
00:03:00,620 --> 00:03:04,310
A cuboid has a volume of 420 cubic centimetres.

51
00:03:04,360 --> 00:03:07,810
Its base is 10 centimetres by 7 centimetres.

52
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:09,640
Find its height.

53
00:03:09,690 --> 00:03:14,250
The area of the base is 10 times 7, which is 70.

54
00:03:14,300 --> 00:03:17,720
Divide the volume by the area of the base.

55
00:03:17,770 --> 00:03:19,620
420 divided by 70 is 6.

56
00:03:19,670 --> 00:03:22,480
The height is 6 centimetres.

57
00:03:22,530 --> 00:03:31,670
How many cubes, each with sides of 3 centimetres, fit in a box 12 centimetres by 9 centimetres by 6 centimetres?

58
00:03:31,720 --> 00:03:33,510
Divide each length by 3.

59
00:03:33,560 --> 00:03:36,960
4 cubes fit along, 3 across, and 2 up.

60
00:03:37,010 --> 00:03:40,350
4 times 3 times 2 is 24 cubes.

61
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:46,000
Find the volume of a cuboid, 6 centimetres by 5 centimetres by 2 centimetres.

62
00:03:46,050 --> 00:03:47,680
Here is Enzo's working.

63
00:03:47,730 --> 00:03:50,280
Which line goes wrong first?

64
00:03:50,330 --> 00:03:51,390
Line 1 is wrong.

65
00:03:51,440 --> 00:03:53,730
Adding the lengths gives a length.

66
00:03:53,780 --> 00:03:56,490
For a volume, you must multiply.

67
00:03:56,540 --> 00:04:00,880
6 times 5 times 2 is 60 cubic centimetres.

68
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:04,020
An Olympic swimming pool is a giant cuboid.

69
00:04:04,070 --> 00:04:09,320
It is 50 metres long, 25 metres wide, and at least 2 metres deep.

70
00:04:09,370 --> 00:04:14,450
50 times 25 times 2 is 2,500 cubic metres.

71
00:04:14,500 --> 00:04:17,000
1 cubic metre holds 1,000 litres.

72
00:04:17,050 --> 00:04:21,800
So the pool holds 2 and a half million litres of water.

73
00:04:21,850 --> 00:04:23,170
One last question.

74
00:04:23,220 --> 00:04:29,260
What is the volume of a cuboid 2 centimetres by 3 centimetres by 10 centimetres?

75
00:04:29,310 --> 00:04:32,710
Pause the video and answer.

76
00:04:32,760 --> 00:04:36,130
2 times 3 is 6, and 6 times 10 is 60.

77
00:04:36,180 --> 00:04:39,920
The volume is 60 cubic centimetres.

78
00:04:39,970 --> 00:04:46,150
To remember: the volume of a cuboid is the length, times the width, times the height.

79
00:04:46,200 --> 00:04:50,960
The volume of a cube is the side, times the side, times the side.

80
00:04:51,010 --> 00:04:55,210
Use the same units, and give the answer in cubic units.

81
00:04:55,260 --> 00:04:59,150
1,000 cubic centimetres is 1 litre.

82
00:04:59,200 --> 00:05:04,510
Great work! Now practise lesson 15.4 on Camday.
